 <title>Climate science and the political compass</title>
 <link>https://coveredinbees.org.archived.website/node/267</link>
 <description>&lt;p&gt;In all my banging on about good science yesterday, I realise on one thing I was being unscientific. A couple of links, to &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.nextleft.org/2009/10/help-can-anyone-find-tory-blogger-who.html?utm_source=Left+Foot+Forward+List&amp;amp;utm_campaign=c30984d1b9-Left_Foot_Forward8_18_2009&amp;amp;utm_medium=email&quot;&gt;Next Left&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;http://crookedtimber.org/2008/06/15/libertarians-and-global-warming/&quot;&gt;Crooked Timber&lt;/a&gt;, wondered why there seemed to be such an anti-AGW consensus on the right. I speculated it may have something to do with a different assessment of the risks - but this is missing a basic question that could be asked. I&#039;ll ask it now, and then suggest that it doesn&#039;t matter anyway.&lt;/p&gt;
